","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":8,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["geo","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2023-10-31","dateOfCreation":"2023-10-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2023-10-17","firstPublicationDate":"2023-10-17","firstIndexDate":"2023-10-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2023-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40820542","source":"MED","pmid":"40820542","pmcid":"PMC12455280","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12455280"]},"doi":"10.1002/bies.70055","title":"The Inner Nuclear Membrane Has a Unique Lipid Signature.","authorString":"Niu Y, Balla T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Niu Y","lastName":"Niu","firstName":"Yang","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-9445-6266"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"International Center for Aging and Cancer, Hainan Medical University, Haikou, Hainan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Balla T","lastName":"Balla","firstName":"Tamas","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Section on Molecular Signal Transduction, Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-9445-6266"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"47","journalIssueId":4020486,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bioessays","title":"BioEssays : news and reviews in molecular, cellular and developmental biology","essn":"1521-1878","issn":"0265-9247","nlmid":"8510851","isoabbreviation":"Bioessays"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-10-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e70055","abstractText":"Although the inner nuclear membrane (INM) is generally considered to be continuous with the outer nuclear membrane (ONM) and connected to the remaining endoplasmic reticulum (ER), it has been well recognized that it is functionally distinct, having a unique protein composition. It has increasingly been recognized, however, that the INM also differs from the ONM and the other ER domains in its lipid composition. It is an intriguing proposition that the unique lipid profile of the INM is intricately linked to its specialized functions related to the nuclear events. Despite rapid progress in recent years in our understanding of the unique lipid profile of the INM and its role in the control of nuclear functions, there is a lot that remains to be understood. This review summarizes recent advances in characterizing the INM lipid composition and lipid synthetic pathways including their possible roles in the control of nuclear functions. Additionally, it discusses current challenges and areas deserving further investigation.","affiliation":"International Center for Aging and Cancer, Hainan Medical University, Haikou, Hainan, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Hainan Medical University","orderIn":0},{"agency":"International Center for Aging and Cancer","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Intramural Research Program of the Eunice Kennedy Shriver National Institute of Child Health and Human Development of the National Institutes of Health of the United States","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nuclear Envelope","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"major