":"2023-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2023-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2023-05-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2023-05-18","firstPublicationDate":"2023-05-18","firstIndexDate":"2023-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"33494405","source":"MED","pmid":"33494405","pmcid":"PMC7865462","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC7865462"]},"doi":"10.3390/ijms22031061","title":"Potential Physiological Relevance of ERAD to the Biosynthesis of GPI-Anchored Proteins in Yeast.","authorString":"Nakatsukasa K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nakatsukasa K","lastName":"Nakatsukasa","firstName":"Kunio","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, Nagoya City University, Yamanohata 1, Mizuho-cho, Mizuho-ku, Nagoya, Aichi 467-8501, Japan."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"22","journalIssueId":3099283,"dateOfPublication":"2021 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Mol Sci","title":"International journal of molecular sciences","essn":"1422-0067","issn":"1422-0067","nlmid":"101092791","isoabbreviation":"Int J Mol Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-01-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"1061","abstractText":"Misfolded and/or unassembled secretory and membrane proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) may be retro-translocated into the cytoplasm, where they undergo ER-associated degradation, or ERAD. The mechanisms by which misfolded proteins are recognized and degraded through this pathway have been studied extensively; however, our understanding of the physiological role of ERAD remains limited. This review describes the biosynthesis and quality control of glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored proteins and briefly summarizes the relevance of ERAD to these processes. While recent studies suggest that ERAD functions as a fail-safe mechanism for the degradation of misfolded GPI-anchored proteins, several pieces of evidence suggest an intimate interaction between ERAD and the biosynthesis of GPI-anchored proteins.","affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, Nagoya City University, Yamanohata 1, Mizuho-cho, Mizuho-ku, Nagoya, Aichi 467-8501, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Toyoaki Scholarship Foundation","grantId":"N/A","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"19H02923","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"18K19306","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"15K18503","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Toray Science Foundation","grantId":"17-5808","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Protein Biosynthesis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biosynthetic Pathways"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"GPI-Linked Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"BI","qualifierName":"biosynthesis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Endoplasmic Reticulum-Associated Degradation"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Saccharomyces cerevisiae","GPI-anchored protein","ERAD","Ubc7","Hrd1","Doa10","Ca2+/mn2+ P-type Atpase","Ubc6"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"GPI-Linked Proteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/22/3/1061/pdf?version=1611376308"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms22031061"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7865462"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7865462?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":7,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2021-09-06","dateOfCreation":"2021-01-26","dateOfRevision":"2021-09-06","electronicPublicationDate":"2021-01-21","firstPublicationDate":"2021-01-21","firstIndexDate":"2021-01-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"34790656","source":"MED","pmid":"34790656","pmcid":"PMC8591177","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC8591177"]},"doi":"10.3389/fcell.2021.720536","title":"The Glycosylphosphatidylinositol Anchor: A Linchpin for Cell Surface Versatility of Trypanosomatids.","authorString":"Borges AR, Link F, Engstler M, Jones NG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Borges AR","lastName":"Borges","firstName":"Alyssa R","initials":"AR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell and Developmental Biology, Biocenter, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Link F","lastName":"Link","firstName":"Fabian","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9828-2012"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell and Developmental Biology, Biocenter, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Engstler M","lastName":"Engstler","firstName":"Markus","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell and Developmental Biology, Biocenter, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Jones NG","lastName":"Jones","firstName":"Nicola G","initials":"NG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3384-0156"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell and Developmental Biology, Biocenter, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2359-1371"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9828-2012"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3384-0156"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":3109504,"dateOfPublication":"2021 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Cell Dev Biol","title":"Frontiers in cell and developmental biology","essn":"2296-634X","issn":"2296-634X","nlmid":"101630250","isoabbreviation":"Front Cell Dev Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-01-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"720536","abstractText":"The use of glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) to anchor proteins to the cell surface is widespread among eukaryotes. The GPI-anchor is covalently attached to the C-terminus of a protein and mediates the protein's attachment to the outer leaflet of the lipid bilayer. GPI-anchored proteins have a wide range of functions, including acting as receptors, transporters, and adhesion molecules. In unicellular eukaryotic parasites, abundantly expressed GPI-anchored proteins are major virulence factors, which support infection and survival within distinct host environments. While, for example, the variant surface glycoprotein (VSG) is the major component of the cell surface of the bloodstream form of African trypanosomes, procyclin is the most abundant protein of the procyclic form which is found in the invertebrate host, the tsetse fly vector. <i>Trypanosoma cruzi</i>, on the other hand, expresses a variety of GPI-anchored molecules on their cell surface, such as mucins, that interact with their hosts. The latter is also true for <i>Leishmania</i>, which use GPI anchors to display, amongst others, lipophosphoglycans on their surface. Clearly, GPI-anchoring is a common feature in trypanosomatids and the fact that it has been maintained throughout eukaryote evolution indicates its adaptive value. Here, we explore and discuss GPI anchors as universal evolutionary building blocks that support the great variety of surface molecules of trypanosomatids.","affiliation":"Department of Cell and Developmental Biology, Biocenter, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Coordenação de Aperfeiçoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Evolution","Leishmania","Trypanosoma","Cell Surface Proteome","Gpi-anchor","Kinetoplastea"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fcell.2021.720536"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8591177"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8591177?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":34,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["uniprot","pdb","doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2021-11-18","dateOfRevision":"2021-11-19","electronicPublicationDate":"2021-11-01","firstPublicationDate":"2021-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2021-11-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"31319476","source":"MED","pmid":"31319476","pmcid":"PMC6678536","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6678536"]},"doi":"10.3390/ijms20143506","title":"Endoplasmic Reticulum Export of GPI-Anchored Proteins.","authorString":"Lopez S, Rodriguez-Gallardo S, Sabido-Bozo S, Muñiz M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lopez S","lastName":"Lopez","firstName":"Sergio","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5952-3568"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, University of Seville, 41012 Seville, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Rodriguez-Gallardo S","lastName":"Rodriguez-Gallardo","firstName":"Sofia","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5189-5671"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, University of Seville, 41012 Seville, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Sabido-Bozo S","lastName":"Sabido-Bozo","firstName":"Susana","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5753-2505"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, University of Seville, 41012 Seville, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Muñiz M","lastName":"Muñiz","firstName":"Manuel","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8011-6991"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, University of Seville, 41012 Seville, Spain. mmuniz@us.es."},{"affiliation":"Instituto de Biomedicina de Sevilla (IBiS), Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío/CSIC/Universidad de Sevilla, 41012 Seville, Spain. mmuniz@us.es."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5952-3568"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8011-6991"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5189-5671"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5753-2505"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"14","volume":"20","journalIssueId":2838028,"dateOfPublication":"2019 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2019,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Mol Sci","title":"International journal of molecular sciences","essn":"1422-0067","issn":"1422-0067","nlmid":"101092791","isoabbreviation":"Int J Mol Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2019-07-01"},"pubYear":"2019","pageInfo":"E3506","abstractText":"Protein export from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is an essential process in all eukaryotes driven by the cytosolic coat complex COPII, which forms vesicles at ER exit sites for transport of correctly assembled secretory cargo to the Golgi apparatus. The COPII machinery must adapt to the existing wide variety of different types of cargo proteins and to different cellular needs for cargo secretion. The study of the ER export of glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored proteins (GPI-APs), a special glycolipid-linked class of cell surface proteins, is contributing to address these key issues. Due to their special biophysical properties, GPI-APs use a specialized COPII machinery to be exported from the ER and their processing and maturation has been recently shown to actively regulate COPII function. In this review, we discuss the regulatory mechanisms by which GPI-APs are assembled and selectively exported from the ER.","affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, University of Seville, 41012 Seville, Spain.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["r