12,"yearOfPublication":2020,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Sci Adv","title":"Science advances","essn":"2375-2548","issn":"2375-2548","nlmid":"101653440","isoabbreviation":"Sci Adv"},"printPublicationDate":"2020-12-01"},"pubYear":"2020","pageInfo":"eaba8237","abstractText":"Protein sorting in the secretory pathway is crucial to maintain cellular compartmentalization and homeostasis. In addition to coat-mediated sorting, the role of lipids in driving protein sorting during secretory transport is a longstanding fundamental question that still remains unanswered. Here, we conduct 3D simultaneous multicolor high-resolution live imaging to demonstrate in vivo that newly synthesized glycosylphosphatidylinositol-anchored proteins having a very long chain ceramide lipid moiety are clustered and sorted into specialized endoplasmic reticulum exit sites that are distinct from those used by transmembrane proteins. Furthermore, we show that the chain length of ceramide in the endoplasmic reticulum membrane is critical for this sorting selectivity. Our study provides the first direct in vivo evidence for lipid chain length-based protein cargo sorting into selective export sites of the secretory pathway.","affiliation":"Department of Cell Biology, Faculty of Biology, University of Seville and Instituto de Biomedicina de Sevilla (IBiS), Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío/CSIC/Universidad de Sevilla, 41012 Seville, Spain.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Swiss National Supercomputing Centre (CSCS) under project ID","grantId":"s842","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Swiss National Science Foundation","acronym":"SNSF_","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"JP17H06420","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Swiss National Supercomputing Centre (CSCS) under project ID","grantId":"s726","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Swiss National Science Foundation","grantId":"184949","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCCR Chemical Biology","grantId":"184949, 185898","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Ministerio de Economía y Competitividad","grantId":"BFU2017-89700-P","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"JP19H02922","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"JP25221103","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Swiss National Science Foundation","acronym":"SNSF_","grantId":"163966","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","grantId":"JP18H05275","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Endoplasmic Reticulum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Ceramides","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Membrane Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Transport"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Secretory Pathway"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Membrane Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Ceramides","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aba8237"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7732199"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7732199?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":51,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2022-04-15","dateOfCreation":"2020-12-14","dateOfRevision":"2025-05-30","electronicPublicationDate":"2020-12-11","firstPublicationDate":"2020-12-11","firstIndexDate":"2020-12-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"30659098","source":"MED","pmid":"30659098","pmcid":"PMC6422101","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6422101"]},"doi":"10.1074/jbc.ra118.003890","title":"The natural anticancer agent cantharidin alters GPI-anchored protein sorting by targeting Cdc1-mediated remodeling in endoplasmic reticulum.","authorString":"Sahu PK, Tomar RS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sahu PK","lastName":"Sahu","firstName":"Pushpendra Kumar","initials":"PK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Laboratory of Chromatin Biology, Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Science Education and Research Bhopal, 462066 Madhya Pradesh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Tomar RS","lastName":"Tomar","firstName":"Raghuvir Singh","initials":"RS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3574-7839"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Laboratory of Chromatin Biology, Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Science Education and Research Bhopal, 462066 Madhya Pradesh, India rst@iiserb.ac.in."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3574-7839"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"294","journalIssueId":2794463,"dateOfPublication":"2019 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2019,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","nlmid":"2985121R","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem"},"printPublicationDate":"2019-03-01"},"pubYear":"2019","pageInfo":"3837-3852","abstractText":"Cantharidin (CTD) is a potent anticancer small molecule produced by several species of blister beetle. It has been a traditional medicine for the management of warts and tumors for many decades. CTD suppresses tumor growth by inducing apoptosis, cell cycle arrest, and DNA damage and inhibits protein phosphatase 2 phosphatase activator (PP2A) and protein phosphatase 1 (PP1). CTD also alters lipid homeostasis, cell wall integrity, endocytosis, adhesion, and invasion in yeast cells. In this study, we identified additional molecular targets of CTD using a <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> strain that expresses a cantharidin resistance gene (<i>CRG1</i>), encoding a SAM-dependent methyltransferase that methylates and inactivates CTD. We found that CTD specifically affects phosphatidylethanolamine (PE)-associated functions that can be rescued by supplementing the growth media with ethanolamine (ETA). CTD also perturbed endoplasmic reticulum (ER) homeostasis and cell wall integrity by altering the sorting of glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored proteins. A CTD-dependent genetic interaction profile of <i>CRG1</i> revealed that the activity of the lipid phosphatase cell division control protein 1 (Cdc1) in GPI-anchor remodeling is the key target of CTD, independently of PP2A and PP1 activities. Moreover, experiments with human cells further suggested that CTD functions through a conserved mechanism in higher eukaryotes. Altogether, we conclude that CTD induces cytotoxicity by targeting Cdc1 activity in GPI-anchor remodeling in the ER.","affiliation":"From the Laboratory of Chromatin Biology, Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Science Education and Research Bhopal, 462066 Madhya Pradesh, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel"